---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BookshelfIllustration from '../components/BookshelfIllustration.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Why Mighty Little Bookshelf exists and how we choose the books in our library."
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 pb-8 pt-10 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="grid items-center gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[1.2fr,0.8fr]">
      <div>
        <p class="section-label">About Us</p>
        <h1 class="mt-2 font-display text-3xl font-semibold sm:text-4xl">
          Books that meet families right where they are.
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-xl text-ink-light">
          Mighty Little Bookshelf is a curated, searchable library of children's books for families navigating the
          NICU, hospital stays, medical conditions, and the big feelings that come with them.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="hidden lg:block">
        <BookshelfIllustration class="h-40 w-full" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 pb-16 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="card space-y-6 p-6 sm:p-10">
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-teal-700">Our Mission</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink">
          A medical diagnosis, a NICU stay, or a hospital admission can turn a family's world upside down overnight,
          and it's often hardest to explain what's happening to the youngest members of the family. We believe the
          right book at the right moment can help a child feel less afraid, help a sibling feel included, and help
          a parent find words they didn't know how to say.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink">
          Our mission is simple: make those books easier to find.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-teal-700">Who's Behind This</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink">
          Mighty Little Bookshelf was created by <strong>Scott McClenahan, RN</strong>, a NICU nurse who has cared
          for babies at every level of neonatal intensive care in hospitals across the country, and his wife,
          <strong>Emily McClenahan</strong>, a cybersecurity specialist and lifelong lover of books, reading, and
          libraries.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink">
          Scott's experience at the bedside helped identify the need. Emily's love of books and libraries helped
          shape the idea of turning that need into a resource families could actually use.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink">
          Mighty Little Bookshelf brings together two things that have always been a big part of our lives: caring
          for people and a love of books.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink">
          My mom was an RN her entire career, so nursing was always somewhere in the background growing up. But it
          wasn't the plan, until it was.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink">
          I graduated from high school in 2000 and took a few college classes while trying to figure out what came
          next. Then 9/11 happened. Like it did for a lot of people, that day changed the direction of my life. I
          decided to join the Marine Corps, and four days later I was on my way to boot camp.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink">
          I served as a Logistics/Embarkation Specialist (MOS 0431), planning and coordinating the movement of
          Marines and equipment by ship, aircraft, and tactical vehicle.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink">
          When my time in the Marines ended, I used my military education benefits to go to nursing school, first
          becoming an LPN and then earning my RN. In 2017, I found my specialty in the NICU.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink">
          Since then, I've chosen to work as a NICU nurse across the United States. My career has taken me from
          Anchorage, Alaska, to Phoenix, Arizona, to Lebanon, New Hampshire, along with hospitals closer to home in
          Arkansas and across the region. I've cared for babies in many different hospital systems and at every
          level of neonatal care, from Level II special care nurseries to Level IV regional referral centers.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink">
          Working in so many different NICUs has given me a perspective I might never have gained by spending my
          entire career in one hospital. I've seen firsthand how differently NICUs care for and educate families,
          what resources parents and siblings are given to help them understand what's happening, and, just as
          importantly, what they aren't given.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink">
          Mighty Little Bookshelf grew out of that gap.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink">
          At nearly every hospital I've worked in, a family would ask me if I knew of a book about the specific
          reason their child was admitted. For an early NICU stay, I usually had an answer. There are a handful of
          good books written for that first admission.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink">
          But NICU stays often stretch into weeks or months, and as they did, parents' questions kept growing past
          what those early books could cover. A baby's medical needs become more specific. There may be surgery,
          respiratory support, a feeding tube, a heart condition, or a genetic diagnosis. Brothers and sisters at
          home start asking more specific questions, too.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink">
          I'd refer families to our child life specialists and other resources, but I often felt like I was sending
          them off without a real answer to the question they were asking: "Is there a book that can help my child
          understand this?"
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink">
          Mighty Little Bookshelf is the answer I wish I'd had for them.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-teal-700">How We Choose Books</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink">
          Every title in our library is reviewed for relevance to a specific medical experience, including NICU and
          prematurity, sibling support, hospital stays, respiratory and cardiac conditions, feeding tubes, oncology,
          genetics and rare diseases, grief and loss, and resources for parents and caregivers.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink">
          Our goal isn't simply to build a long list of children's books. It's to make that list genuinely useful
          when a family needs it.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink">
          We're steadily verifying and expanding the details on every listing, including age range, format, subject
          matter, and a full description. As the library grows, you may see a "verification in progress" note on
          newer additions while that information is being reviewed.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-teal-700">How this site supports itself</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink">
          Mighty Little Bookshelf participates in the Amazon Associates program. When you buy a book through a link
          on our site, we may earn a small commission at no extra cost to you. Read our full
          <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/" class="font-semibold text-teal-700 underline">affiliate disclosure</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-teal-700">Have a book to recommend?</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink">
          We'd love to hear from you. <a href="/contact/" class="font-semibold text-teal-700 underline">Get in touch</a>
          with a title, and we'll add it to our review list.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
